作者: 斯坦福(stefan)
题目: mirror and server relocation
日期: Sat Nov  6 23:49:47 1999
I am having trouble with the new server I mentioned last time, too.
If someone volunteer to host sanguo's server, it would be great.
However, there are things I am worrying about:

1. how secure is that server, not in terms of system security, but also the
people who manage it. So far, I am quite certain that nobody has ever got
a complete copy of our lib yet. But if somebody we never dealt with is
going to manage it, I am quite worried.

2. It is bad for the mud that the development gets branched, especially those
libs got pirated. Look at XKX back three years ago, how many people
have got the pirated copy and declare they own it and go on their
own branch of developing a mud. Few has been successful. 

3. We are not ready to release a version yet. Not to mention the bugs that
players are complaining about, but the main structures have not been 
settled yet. If we allow branching of development, we will
eventually face the incompatibility of all sorts. People will 
just hijack our code and declare they own it, it is their own sanguo :)
Just look at XKX, US XKX and China XKX are different.

4. An open source development group is the way to go. If we are not
afraid of releasing the secrets in the mudlib, we can build a webpage
and start to organize development over the internet, encourage
people to contribute their own code to the library.

Pretty much as I prefer number 4 as the future direction, but I am
afraid a mudlib is not suitable to be completely open, because people will
study the code and know all the secrets. I believe SanGuo is already
much different from traditional role playing muds. We do not have
many secrets to hide away from normal players. We set off with a goal
to build a dynamic mud.

I am trying to re-build our web page. If we are really going to
open up source codes, I think we should open up base lib first
before we open up SanGuo specific world source. How does that sound
to everyone? Lots of efforts are needed to accomplish this
goal though.

For mirrors, once we open up, there can be as many as they request.
As long as we reach an agreement that all development work based
on our source code should also be open to public, SanGuo lib will
live on forever :)


